&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h1 id=&#34;what-is-openhuman&#34;&gt;What is OpenHuman?
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;OpenHuman is an open-source agentic assistant designed to integrate with you in your daily life. Each bullet links to the deeper writeup in the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;docs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Simple, UI-first &amp;amp; Human&lt;/strong&gt; A clean desktop experience and short onboarding paths take you from install to a working agent in a few clicks — no config-first setup, no terminal required. The agent has &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/mascot&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;a face&lt;/a&gt;: a desktop mascot that speaks, reacts to its surroundings, &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/mascot/meeting-agents&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;joins your Google Meets&lt;/a&gt; as a real participant, remembers you across weeks, and keeps thinking in the background even when you&amp;rsquo;ve stopped typing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;118+ third-party integrations&lt;/a&gt; with &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/obsidian-wiki/auto-fetch&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;auto-fetch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: plug into Gmail, Notion, GitHub, Slack, Stripe, Calendar, Drive, Linear, Jira and the rest of your stack with &lt;strong&gt;one-click OAuth&lt;/strong&gt;. Every connection is exposed to the agent as a typed tool, and every twenty minutes the core walks each active connection and pulls fresh data into the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations/auto-fetch&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;memory tree&lt;/a&gt;. No prompts, no polling loops you have to write, so the agent already has tomorrow&amp;rsquo;s context this morning.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/memory-tree&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Memory Tree&lt;/a&gt; + &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/obsidian-wiki&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Obsidian Wiki&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: a local-first knowledge base built from your data and your activity. Everything you connect is canonicalized into ≤3k-token Markdown chunks, scored, and folded into hierarchical summary trees stored in &lt;strong&gt;SQLite on your machine&lt;/strong&gt;. The same chunks land as &lt;code&gt;.md&lt;/code&gt; files in an Obsidian-compatible vault you can open, browse and edit, inspired by Karpathy&amp;rsquo;s &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://x.com/karpathy/status/2039805659525644595&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;obsidian-wiki workflow&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Batteries included&lt;/strong&gt;: web search, a web-fetch &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/native-tools&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;scraper&lt;/a&gt;, a full coder toolset (filesystem, git, lint, test, grep), and &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/voice&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;native voice&lt;/a&gt; (STT in, ElevenLabs TTS out, mascot lip-sync, live Google Meet agent) are wired in by default. &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/model-routing&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Model routing&lt;/a&gt; sends each task to the right LLM (reasoning, fast, or vision) under one subscription. No &amp;ldquo;install a plugin to read files&amp;rdquo; friction. &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/model-routing/local-ai&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Optional local AI via Ollama&lt;/a&gt; for on-device workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/token-compression&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Smart token compression (TokenJuice)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: every tool call, scrape result, email body, and search payload is run through a token compression layer before it touches any LLM Model. HTML is converted to Markdown, long URLs are shortened, and verbose tool output is deduped and summarized via a configurable rule overlay etc&amp;hellip; CJK, emoji, and other multi-byte text are preserved grapheme-by-grapheme — never stripped. You get the same information but at a fraction of the tokens. Reducing cost &amp;amp; latency by up to 80%.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations#messaging-channels&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Messaging channels&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/privacy-and-security&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;privacy &amp;amp; security&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: inbound/outbound across the channels you already use, with workflow data that stays on device, encrypted locally, treated as yours.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;contributing-from-source&#34;&gt;Contributing from source
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;New contributor? Start with &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;./CONTRIBUTING.md&#34; &gt;&lt;code&gt;CONTRIBUTING.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for the fork/PR workflow and local validation commands, or use the copy-paste AI-agent prompt in &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;./CONTRIBUTING-BEGINNERS.md#optional-let-an-ai-coding-agent-guide-you&#34; &gt;&lt;code&gt;CONTRIBUTING-BEGINNERS.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The short path is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install Git, Node.js 24+, pnpm 10.10.0, Rust 1.93.0 (&lt;code&gt;rustfmt&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;clippy&lt;/code&gt;), CMake, Ninja, ripgrep, and the platform desktop build prerequisites.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fork and clone the repo, then run &lt;code&gt;git submodule update --init --recursive&lt;/code&gt; before &lt;code&gt;pnpm install&lt;/code&gt; so the vendored Tauri/CEF sources are present.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;pnpm dev&lt;/code&gt; for web-only UI work, &lt;code&gt;pnpm --filter openhuman-app dev:app&lt;/code&gt; for the desktop shell, and focused checks such as &lt;code&gt;pnpm typecheck&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;pnpm format:check&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;cargo check -p openhuman --lib&lt;/code&gt; before opening a PR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Deeper docs: &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/architecture&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Architecture&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/developing/getting-set-up&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Getting Set Up&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;./gitbooks/features/cloud-deploy.md&#34; &gt;Cloud Deploy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;context-in-minutes-not-weeks&#34;&gt;Context in minutes, not weeks
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;OpenHuman is the first agent harness that gets to know you in minutes. Inspired by &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://x.com/karpathy/status/2039805659525644595&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Karpathy&amp;rsquo;s LLM Knowledgebase&lt;/a&gt;. Most agents start cold. Hermes learns by watching you work; OpenClaw waits for plugins to ferry context in. Either way, you spend days or weeks before the agent knows enough about your stack to be genuinely useful.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p align=&#34;center&#34;&gt;
 &lt;img src=&#34;./gitbooks/.gitbook/assets/image (1).png&#34; alt=&#34;OpenHuman context diagram&#34;&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenHuman summarizes and compresses all your documents, emails &amp;amp; chats; and creates a memory graph that lets your agent remember everything about you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenHuman skips the wait. Connect your accounts, let &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/integrations/auto-fetch&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;auto-fetch&lt;/a&gt; pull data locally on a 20-minute loop, and then have &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/memory-tree&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Memory Trees&lt;/a&gt; compress everything into Markdown files stored intelligently in a &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/obsidian-wiki&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Karpathy-style Obsidian wiki&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In just one sync pass, the agent has full (compressed) context of your inbox, your calendar, your repos, your docs, your messages. No training period. No &amp;ldquo;give it a few weeks.&amp;rdquo;. It becomes you, controlled by you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Already self-host &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/rohitg00/agentmemory&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;agentmemory&lt;/a&gt; across other coding agents? OpenHuman ships an optional &lt;code&gt;Memory&lt;/code&gt; backend that proxies to it — set &lt;code&gt;memory.backend = &amp;quot;agentmemory&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;config.toml&lt;/code&gt; and the same durable store powers OpenHuman alongside Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and OpenCode. See the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://tinyhumans.gitbook.io/openhuman/features/obsidian-wiki/agentmemory-backend&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;agentmemory backend&lt;/a&gt; page for setup.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;openhuman-vs-other-agent-harnesses&#34;&gt;OpenHuman vs Other Agent Harnesses
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;High-level comparison (products evolve, so verify against each vendor). OpenHuman is built to &lt;strong&gt;minimize vendor sprawl&lt;/strong&gt;, keep &lt;strong&gt;workflow knowledge on-device&lt;/strong&gt;, and give the agent a &lt;strong&gt;persistent memory&lt;/strong&gt; of your data, not only chat.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
	&lt;thead&gt;
			&lt;tr&gt;
					&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
					&lt;th&gt;Claude Cowork&lt;/th&gt;
					&lt;th&gt;OpenClaw&lt;/th&gt;
					&lt;th&gt;Hermes Agent&lt;/th&gt;
					&lt;th&gt;OpenHuman&lt;/th&gt;
			&lt;/tr&gt;
	&lt;/thead&gt;
	&lt;tbody&gt;
			&lt;tr&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Open-source&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;🚫 Proprietary&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ MIT&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ MIT&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ GNU&lt;/td&gt;
			&lt;/tr&gt;
			&lt;tr&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Simple to start&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ Desktop + CLI&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;⚠️ Terminal-first&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;⚠️ Terminal-first&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ Clean UI, minutes&lt;/td&gt;
			&lt;/tr&gt;
			&lt;tr&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cost&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;⚠️ Sub + add-ons&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;⚠️ BYO models&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;⚠️ BYO models&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ One sub + TokenJuice&lt;/td&gt;
			&lt;/tr&gt;
			&lt;tr&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
					&lt;td&gt;✅ Chat-scoped&lt;/td&gt;
